    kg:docId "2403.11340_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2403_11340_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "diffusion probabilistic model (DDPM) defines the diffusion process as a two-stage process, i.e., forward and reverse. The forward process progressively corrupts the image, I 0 R H W , by adding Gaussian noise over T iterations in a Markovian manner. A neural network parameterized by , denoted as f ( I t ( I 0 , t ) , t ) , takes the noisy image I t and the current random noise level t as inputs to estimate the noise vector used to corrupt the original image I 0 . The loss function is defined as " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2403_11340_rag__chunk_006> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2403.11340_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "2403.11340_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2403_11340_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "he , I ihc t ( I ihc 0 , t ) , t ) || 2 For a detailed background on diffusion models, please refer to the supplementary materials section 7 . 4. Methods: S TAIN D IFFUSER S TAIN D IFFUSER is trained to minimize the sum of these losses, specifically, L stain = L gen + L seg , where is a hyperparameter that controls the interaction between tasks, regulating their impact on one another. Segmentation Annotations: Manual segmentation of cells or objects significantly increases deployment costs and l" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2403_11340_rag__chunk_008> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2403.11340_rag::chunk_008" ;
    kg:docId "2403.11340_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2403_11340_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "denoising the noisy IHC. In the second diffusion process, the roles of H&E and IHC are reversed. Let input H&E and IHC images be denoted as I he and I ihc , respectively, and noisy H&E and IHC at time step t as I he t and I ihc t , respectively. The final loss used to train the bidirectional model is: Each dataset consists of 92 H&E WSIs paired with corresponding IHC stains (CK8/18 or CD3) from the same tissue samples collected during surveillance colonoscopies of patients with ulcerative coliti" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2403_11340_rag__chunk_010> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2403.11340_rag::chunk_010" ;
    kg:docId "2403.11340_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2403_11340_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "contrast, distribution-based metricssuch as FID, KID, precision, and recalltend to be higher for most unpaired models. This may be because unpaired models are trained on O( N 2 ) sample pairs(due to all combinations of unpaired images), enabling them to capture the datasets latent distribution more effectively. Notably, S TAIN D IFFUSER architectures are the only paired models that match or exceed unpaired models on all distribution metrics. Dataset Size Ablation. Medical datasets are often in s" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2405_10254_rag__chunk_000> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2405.10254_rag::chunk_000" ;
    kg:docId "2405.10254_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2405_10254_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "PRISM: A M ULTI -M ODAL G ENERATIVE F OUNDATION M ODEL FOR S LIDE -L EVEL H ISTOPATHOLOGY A P REPRINT George Shaikovski 1 , Adam Casson 1 , Kristen Severson 2 , Eric Zimmermann 2 , Yi Kan Wang 1 , Jeremy D. Kunz 1 , Juan A. Retamero 1 , Gerard Oakley 1 , David Klimstra 1 , Christopher Kanan 1,3 , Matthew Hanna 1,4 , Michal Zelechowski 1 , Julian Viret 1 , Neil Tenenholtz 2 , James Hall 2 , Nicol`o Fusi 2 , Razik Yousfi 1 , Peter Hamilton 1 , William A. Moye 1 , Eugene Vorontsov 1 , Siqi Liu 1 , " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2405_10254_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2405.10254_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2405.10254_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2405_10254_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "to revolutionize the field of pathology and, ultimately, clinical oncology practices. To build on this progress, recent efforts have focused on creating foundation models capable of operating on tile images (small sub-regions of the WSI), trained with self-supervised learning on datasets ranging from tens of thousands to Corresponding author. siqi.liu AT paige DOT ai These authors contributed equally to this work. Image Perception PRISM Virchow Classifier Biomarker Detection Train a label-effici" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2405_10254_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2405.10254_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "2405.10254_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2405_10254_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "to fully-supervised models. Importantly, we demonstrate that pre-training can also benefit specific downstream tasks not covered by the report text. As clinical reports can be obtained without further expensive manual annotation, the proposed pre-training approach can be easily scaled to millions of WSIs. PRISM is pre-trained using 587 thousand WSIs and 195 thousand associated clinical text reports. The model is capable of generating text-based diagnosis reports for WSIs. While the primary objec" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2405_10254_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2405.10254_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2405.10254_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2405_10254_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "the need to train an aggregator network makes it difficult to substantially improve performance on low data tasks like biomarker prediction. This motivates pre-training of the aggregator network in a more complete foundation model. Some recent works incorporate language for pre-training tile-level models [18, 19, 27, 28]. Gamper et al. [18] encode all tissue tiles with a convolutional neural network (CNN) and pass to a transformer for autoregressive caption decoding. Later methods build on the C" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2407_11664_rag__chunk_000> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2407.11664_rag::chunk_000" ;
