<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_04704_rag__chunk_002> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.04704_rag::chunk_002" ;
    kg:docId "2507.04704_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_04704_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "S PATIA groups neighboring cells into spatial patches (e.g., 256 256 pixels) and applies a transformer to model local cell-cell interactions. At the tissue level , a global transformer aggregates niche representations to capture long-range dependencies across the full slide. S PATIA is trained on MIST (Multi-scale dataset for Image-based Spatial Transcriptomics), a newly assembled multi-level dataset of image-based spatial transcriptomics. MIST (MIST-C-17M, MIST-N-1M, MISTT-10K) contains 17 mill" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_04704_rag__chunk_005> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.04704_rag::chunk_005" ;
    kg:docId "2507.04704_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_04704_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan ", (3) D tissue = { ( T k , g t k , s k ) } Q k =1 , To obtain the final unified embedding for each cell i , we attend from its cell-level representation to both the corresponding niche and the global tissue context: z i = F fusion z c i |{z} cell level R D , (4) , z n j |{z} niche level , z t k |{z} tissue level The resulting unified embedding z i , along with the intermediate scale-specific embeddings z c i , z n i , and z t i , can be leveraged for a wide range of downstream biomedical tasks. " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_04704_rag__chunk_006> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.04704_rag::chunk_006" ;
    kg:docId "2507.04704_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_04704_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "counts or normalized expression values. We process g i using a gene encoder, E gene . We apply the pretrained scPRINT [ 28 ] as the gene backbone to embed the gene expressions. For a panel of N gene genes, we obtain an embedding matrix x i R N gene D , where each row corresponds to a specific genes expression mapped into the latent space, potentially using learnable gene-specific embeddings modulated by expression values. The modality-specific encoders are defined as: X c i = E cell ( C i ) R N " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_04704_rag__chunk_007> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.04704_rag::chunk_007" ;
    kg:docId "2507.04704_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_04704_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "} , we first extract a sequence of morphological tokens X n j = E niche N j R N patch D , where E niche is a ViT-based encoder and N patch is the number of local image patches. In parallel, we gather the unified per-cell embeddings { z c j } j C j and compute their pooled context z k = 1 |C j | P i C j z c i R D . We then fuse morphology and cellular context via cross-attention: z n j = CrossAttn( Q = z k , K = X n j , V = X n j ) R D . (6) The neighbouring morphology tokens X n k serve as the q" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_04704_rag__chunk_008> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.04704_rag::chunk_008" ;
    kg:docId "2507.04704_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_04704_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "annotations and allows the model to learn rich, biologically meaningful representations. We enforce consistency between the modalities and learn the fusion process by reconstructing the original data from the unified embedding via a dual decoder architecture. Image Reconstruction. An image decoder D cell takes the unified embedding z c i as input and aims to reconstruct the original cell image patch C i = D cell ( z c i ) . The reconstruction loss L img_recon is typically the MAE [20] between th" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_04704_rag__chunk_009> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.04704_rag::chunk_009" ;
    kg:docId "2507.04704_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_04704_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "leading to the quality degradation of the synthesized images. These methods typically adopt exclusive pairings, where once a token is assigned to a particular group, it cannot be reassigned to other groups for merging. This constraint limits the models representational power, since a single token might be relevant to multiple groups concurrently. To tackle these problems, we first partition the token sequence of length N based on token importance, using classifier-free guidance (CFG) [ 23 ] as a" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_04704_rag__chunk_010> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.04704_rag::chunk_010" ;
    kg:docId "2507.04704_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_04704_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "image patch and its aggregated gene profile. Finally, MIST-T summarizes each slide by its set of niche embeddings and positional metadata, with a size of 1024 1024 , enabling tissue-level tasks such as global composition prediction and cross-slide transfer. All three scales are provided with consistent preprocessing, enabling end-to-end evaluation of cell-, niche-, and tissue-level tasks for machine learning research. The full dataset statistics are present in Appendix A. Baselines. We benchmark" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_06418_rag__chunk_000> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.06418_rag::chunk_000" ;
    kg:docId "2507.06418_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_06418_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "PAST: A multimodal single-cell foundation model for histopathology and spatial transcriptomics in cancer Changchun Yang 1,2,3* , Haoyang Li 1,2,3* , Yushuai Wu 4,5 , Yilan Zhang 1,2,3 , Yifeng Jiao 4,5 , Yu Zhang 4,5 , Rihan Huang 1,2,3 , Yuan Cheng 4,5# , Yuan Qi 4,5,6# , Xin Guo 4,5# and Xin Gao 1,2,3,4# 1 Computer Science Program, Computer, Electrical and Mathematical Sciences and Engineering Division, King Abdullah University of Science and Technology (KAUST), Thuwal 23955-6900, Kingdom of S" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_06418_rag__chunk_008> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.06418_rag::chunk_008" ;
    kg:docId "2507.06418_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_06418_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "the MCAT multimodal survival framework, we show that PAST achieves the highest average performance across six TCGA cohorts and delivers the best survival prediction in four out of six individual datasets, particularly excelling in heterogeneous tumor types such as LUAD and COADREAD, demonstrating both robust generalization and cohort-specific utility. Furthermore, the addition of spatially resolved single-cell embeddings (PAST-multi) improves censoring-aware performance, as measured by the inver" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_06418_rag__chunk_009> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.06418_rag::chunk_009" ;
    kg:docId "2507.06418_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_06418_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "transformation matrix by Kabsch algorithm [39]. To account for tissue heterogeneity and local deformations, we selected multiple sets of keypoints from distinct regions on each WSI, including areas with high or low cell density, tumor-enriched zones, and non-tumoral regions. The resulting transformation matrices from different keypoint sets were then combined and adjusted to maximize registration accuracy across the slide. This enabled us to robustly align single-cell molecular profiles with the" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_06418_rag__chunk_010> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.06418_rag::chunk_010" ;
    kg:docId "2507.06418_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_06418_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "pixel within the cell mask, and is the total number of pixels in cell . Using this centroid, we extracted a square image patch of size (with =224) centered at from the corresponding H&E-stained whole slide image. All image patches were normalized prior to model input using the standard mean and standard deviation (mean = [0.485, 0.456, 0.406], std = [0.229, 0.224, 0.225]) to ensure consistency and comparability across samples. Cell Encoder Input Preparation: To address differences in sequencing " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2507_14497v2_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2507.14497v2_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2507.14497v2_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2507_14497v2_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "diagnostic information while significantly reducing computational overhead. 1. Introduction Understanding whole slide images (WSIs) at the gigapixel scale has emerged as an increasingly important topic in computational pathology. However, WSIs are typically extremely large, often spanning dimensions of up to 100 , 000 100 , 000 pixels, which poses significant computational challenges for multimodal large language modeling. Most existing computational pathology methods are limited to patch-level " .

