    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1710_08543_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "learning can be applied to train the mapping from gray image to the colored one. We used gray normalization based on Pillow package of python which uses this formula L = 0 . 299 R + 0 . 587 G + 0 . 144 B . 3.2. Network Architecture In this part, we explain each network structure of classier network and stain-style generator which constitute SST network. 6 Neural Stain-Style Transfer Network Figure 4: Comparison between SST and other stain normalization method: (a) Target image for transfer (b) O" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1907_02644_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1907.02644_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "1907.02644_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1907_02644_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "these valuable data largely under-explored. Consequently, cellular behaviours and the tumor microenvironment recorded in H&E images remain poorly understood. Increasing our understanding of such microenvironment interaction holds the key for improved diagnosis and treatment of cancer. Figure 1: (a): Images (224 224) from PathologyGAN trained on H&E breast cancer tissue. (b): Real images, Inception-V1 closest neighbor to the generated above in (a). (c): Images (224 224) from PathologyGAN trained " .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/1907_02644_rag__chunk_007> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "1907.02644_rag::chunk_007" ;
    kg:docId "1907.02644_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/1907_02644_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "to breast cancer tissue, since the tool was developed for this specic case. Figure 3 displays an example of how the CRImage captures the dierent cells in the generated images, such as cancer cells, stromal cells, and lymphocytes. Finally, we created 8 dierent classes that account for counts of cancer cells in the tissue image, and consecutively we label each generated image with the corresponding class, allowing us to more clearly visualize the relation between cancer cell density in generated i" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2003_10286_rag__chunk_001> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2003.10286_rag::chunk_001" ;
    kg:docId "2003.10286_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2003_10286_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "for training data-driven models. Some commercial institutes provide a larger number of practice questions, but they are very expensive to buy and they cannot be shared with the public due to copyright issues. A PREPRINT M ARCH 24, 2020 Q : What are these GMS-stained organisms ? A1 : Blastomyces dermatitidis. A2 : Cryptococcus neoformans. A3 : Pneumocystis jiroveci . A4 : trophozoites of Entamoeba histolytica . A5 : yeasts of Candida species. Figure 2: An example of image/caption pairs from the T" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2003_10286_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2003.10286_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2003.10286_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2003_10286_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "11 types, e.g. modality, plane, etc. 58% of the questions are in MC style and the rest are open-ended. Our dataset differs from VQA-Med and VQA-RAD in two-fold. First, our dataset is about pathology while VQA-Med and VQA-RAD [ 7 ] are both about radiology. Second, our dataset is a truly challenging QA dataset where most of the questions are open-ended while in VQA-Med and VQA-RAD the majority of questions have a xed number of candidate answers and can be answered by multi-way classication. Besid" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2310_20607_rag__chunk_003> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2310.20607_rag::chunk_003" ;
    kg:docId "2310.20607_rag" ;
    kg:inPaper <http://example.org/kg/paper/2310_20607_rag> ;
    kg:textSpan "and predicts the subtype label of the WSI. This module is intended to guide the model to learn more discriminative features that can better capture the subtle differences among different subtypes of cancer. We will provide more details about this module in Section II-B . The redundancy of information in a WSI allows the same information to be likely to appear in multiple patches, so it is possible to use sparse grid features. We propose the Asymmetric Masked Mechanism (AMM) for sparse-grid featu" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2310_20607_rag__chunk_005> a kg:Chunk ;
    kg:chunkId "2310.20607_rag::chunk_005" ;
