    kg:textSpan "1. INTRODUCTION Developing deep learning models in computational pathology requires datasets with accurate, relevant information for tasks like segmentation and object detection. Creating these datasets involves stages such as sample preparation, staining, imaging, and expert manual annotation, which is labor-intensive and costly [1]. In contrast, in-silico data provides a more efficient, cost-effective alternative by generating datasets with the necessary properties through controllable paramet" .

<http://example.org/kg/chunk/2407_18449_rag__chunk_011> a kg:Chunk ;
